  "query_preview": "..Current radar shows a few light showers north of\nTucson in Pinal County. This activity will continue over the\nnext hour or two then dissipate. Aloft high pressure is centered\nover New Mexico and a trough is moving over the west coast. \nOver the weekend the trough will push the ridge further east \nover the Great Plains. Today will be the last day of the Extreme\nHeat Warning with temperatures 4-8 degrees above normal. \nTroughing will stay in place just to the west through the coming\nwork week as well. Deterministic models also show a couple \nshortwaves moving through Arizona this weekend into Monday. \n\nMoisture values will be slightly above normal today into next\nweek and with broad synoptic scale ascent ahead of the west\ncoast trough an uptick in monsoon thunderstorm development is\nexpected through Monday. Today expect scattered thunderstorms \nto begin to develop as early as noon and continue into the \nevening. The 29/00Z HREF shows the potential for storms to \nproduce rainfall rates 1-2 inches per hour with the greatest \nthreat for heavy rainfall from central Pima County to western \nCochise and Graham Counties. This threat also extends into \nSunday for similar areas. Training storms may also be a threat \nwith area washes and rivers expected to be running. Because of \nthis a Flash Flood Watch is in effect through Sunday evening. \nSevere wind gusts will also be a threat with the best chances \nover Pima and Pinal Counties. Outflow generated blowing dust \ncould be a threat Saturday and Sunday evenings in Pinal County. \nNocturnal activity will also be a possibility both Saturday and\nSunday night thanks to small disturbances moving through ahead \nof the main trough. Although Saturday night the latest CAMs \nshow most of the activity north of the CWA with better chances \nSunday night. The nighttime activity could complicate the next \nday's convection, either delaying the start time or even causing\na decrease in coverage. This is the biggest source of uncertainty\nover the next couple of days.\n\nAfter Monday troughing stays in place to the west and over the \nPacific Northwest keeping Arizona under southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow. The ridge stays over the southern Great\nPlains or the Southeast. Moisture values stay slightly above \nnormal, but storm chances decrease as a more transitional \nmonsoon pattern sets up. Still expect daily afternoon and \nevening thunderstorm chances but coverage will likely be reduced\nwith the best chances from Tucson south and eastward. \nTemperatures also decrease to around normal Sunday into the \nmiddle of next week.",
